ove equation by $(x+1)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n3x+1=A(x+1)^{2}+B(x+1)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-1)+1 & =A(-1+1)^{2}+B(-1+1)+C \\\\ -2 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(0)+1 & =A(0+1)^{2}+B(0+1)+C \\\\ 1 & =A+B-2 \\\\ A+B & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=-2$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-2)+1 & =A(-2+1)^{2}+B(-2+1)+C \\\\ -5 & =A-B-2 \\\\ A-B & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations $A-B=-3$ and $A+B=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A & =-3+3 \\\\ 2A & =0 \\\\ A & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into $A+B=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+B & =3 \\\\ B & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+1}{(x+1)^{3}}=\\frac{3}{(x+1)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x+1}{(x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3}{(x+1)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{(x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{x+1}+\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042676/state.json" "file_sha256" "ad4dbd3ec8405462ccdab0221832d812651cfd8e53c0fd49077d50cda99641b1"} {"question" "q-82892" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042676/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2c238dc013e93696dca3a4c4c9ddb4498ed87910f0e9671a8aac9473b41e03cf"} {"question" "q-82892" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042676/state.json" "file_sha256" "ad4dbd3ec8405462ccdab0221832d812651cfd8e53c0fd49077d50cda99641b1"} {"question" "q-82892" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{3}{x+1}+\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}+K"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{x+1}+\\frac{3}{(x+1)^{2}}+K"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+\\frac{2}{x+1}+\\frac{3}{(x+1)^{2}}+K"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|-\\frac{3}{x+1}+\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}+K"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|-\\frac{6}{(x+1)^{2}}+K"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042676/state.json" "file_sha256" "ad4dbd3ec8405462ccdab0221832d812651cfd8e53c0fd49077d50cda99641b1"} {"question" "q-82892" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{3}{x+1}+\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}+K"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042676/state.json" "file_sha256" "ad4dbd3ec8405462ccdab0221832d812651cfd8e53c0fd49077d50cda99641b1"} {"question" "q-115655" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$\\int \\frac{-{x}^{2}+9x-16}{(x-6)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042676/state.json" "file_sha256" "ad4dbd3ec8405462ccdab0221832d812651cfd8e53c0fd49077d50cda99641b1"} {"question" "q-115655" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{-{x}^{2}+9x-16}{(x-6)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{x-6}+\\frac{B}{(x-6)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x-6)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-6)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}+9x-16=A(x-6)^{2}+B(x-6)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=6,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(6)^{2}+9(6)-16 & =A(6-6)^{2}+B(6-6)+C \\\\ 2 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=5$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(5)^{2}+9(5)-16 & =A(5-6)^{2}+B(5-6)+C \\\\ 4 & =A-B+2 \\\\ A-B & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=7$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(7)^{2}+9(7)-16 & =A(7-6)^{2}+B(7-6)+C \\\\ -2 & =A+B+2 \\\\ A+B & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations $A-B=2$ and $A+B=-4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A & =2-4 \\\\ A & =-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nand substituting back into $A+B=-4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1+B & =-4 \\\\ B & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\frac{-{x}^{2}+9x-16}{(x-5)^{3}}=-\\frac{1}{x-6}-\\frac{3}{(x-6)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(x-6)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{-{x}^{2}+9x-16}{(x-6)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{x-6}-\\frac{3}{(x-6)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(x-6)^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x-6}\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int \\frac{1}{(x-6)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{(x-6)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-6|+\\frac{3}{x-6}-\\frac{1}{(x-6)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042676/state.json" "file_sha256" "ad4dbd3ec8405462ccdab0221832d812651cfd8e53c0fd49077d50cda99641b1"} {"question" "q-115655" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042676/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2c238dc013e93696dca3a4c4