rac{B}{(x+1)^2}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x+1)^2$ and get\n\n$$\n2x+3 = A(x+1)+B .\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-1)+3 & =A(-1+1)+B \\\\ 1 & =0+B \\\\ B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)+3 & =A(0+1)+B \\\\ 3 & =A+B \\\\ 3 & =A+1 \\\\ A & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{(x+1)^2} = \\dfrac{2}{(x+1)} +\\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2x+3}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{2}{(x+1)}+\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1||}_{0}^{1}+{(-\\frac{1}{x+1})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1||}_{0}^{1}-{\\frac{1}{x+1}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1]-[\\frac{1}{2}-1] \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042676/example-508.json" "file_sha256" "f81e942ed88052380ab57101989c62f7fb6672476baeaa44f72d25522b73e43d"} {"question" "e-7298" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{3x^2-5x+8}{(x-3)^2(x+1)}\\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042676/example-7298.json" "file_sha256" "fb22ec1d9494132e6064d6daf8dedaa8b57d3952c22fb6ee4bc3681cc5b0f8e2"} {"question" "e-7298" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}-5x+8}{(x-3)^{2}(x+1)} & =\\frac{A}{(x-3)}+\\frac{B}{(x-3)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-3)^2(x+1)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-5x+8 & =A(x-3)(x+1)+B(x+1)+C(x-3)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $C$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({3}^{2})-5(3)+8 & =A(3-3)(3+1)+B(3+1)+C(3-3)^{2} \\\\ 20 & =B(4) \\\\ B & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-1)^{2}-5(-1)+8 & =A(-1-3)(-1+1)+B(-1+1)+C(-1-3)^{2} \\\\ 16 & =C{(-4)}^{2} \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known values of $B$ and $C,$ we can solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({0}^{2})-5(0)+8 & =A(0-3)(0+1)+B(0+1)+C(0-3)^{2} \\\\ 8 & =-3A+B+9C \\\\ 8 & =-3A+5+9(1) \\\\ 3A & =6 \\\\ A & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3x^2-5x+8}{(x-3)^2(x+1)} = \\dfrac{2}{(x-3)} +\\dfrac{5}{(x-3)^2} +\\dfrac{1}{(x+1)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}-5x+8}{(x-3)^{2}(x+1)}\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{(x-3)}+\\frac{5}{(x-3)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(x+1)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\text{d}x+5\\int \\frac{1}{(x-3)^{2}}\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+5(-\\,\\frac{1}{x-3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+K \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|-\\frac{5}{(x-3)}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+K\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042676/example-7298.json" "file_sha256" "fb22ec1d9494132e6064d6daf8dedaa8b57d3952c22fb6ee4bc3681cc5b0f8e2"} {"question" "e-7275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1} \\dfrac{x}{x^2+2x+1} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042676/example-7275.json" "file_sha256" "324327e87dced806cbc3e179a8e388914820956dc0677cf15a5969cead11df45"} {"question" "e-7275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's first factor the denominator of the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{x^2+2x+1} = \\dfrac{x}{(x+1)^2}\n$$\n\nWe must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{(x+1)^2} = \\dfrac {A}{x+1} + \\dfrac {B}{(x+1)^2}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x+1)^2$ and get\n\n$$\nx= A(x+1)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =A(-1+1)+B \\\\ B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =A(0+1)+B \\\\ 0 & =A+B \\\\ 0 & =A-1 \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{(x+1)^2} = \\dfrac {1}{x+1} - \\dfrac {1}{(x+1)^2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{x+1}-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|{|}_{0}^{1}+{\\frac{1}{x+1}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|0+1|)+(\\frac{1}{1+1}-\\frac{1}{0+1}) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)+(\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042676/example-7275.json" "file_sha256" "324327e87dced806cbc3e179a8e388914820956dc0677cf15a5969cead11df45"} {"question" "e-509" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{9x^2+2x-1}{(3x-1)^3}\\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042676/example-509.json" "file_sha256" "c1b4a3a046420671952d88ed307528ee3fdeab7ee40cb620264db559f1299af7"} {"question" "e-509" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9{x}^{2}+2x-1}{(3x-1)^{3}} & =\\frac{A}{(3x-1)}+\\frac{B}{(3x-1)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(3x-1)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(3x-1)^3$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}+2x-1 & =A(3x-1)^{2}+B(3x-1)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=\\dfrac{1}{3},$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+2(\\frac{1}{3})-1 & =A{(3(\\frac{1}{3})-1)}^{2}+B(3(\\frac{1}{3})-1)+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{(0)}^{2}+2(0)-1 & =A{(0-1)}^{2}+B(0-1)+C \\\\ -1 & =A-B+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ A-B & =-\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get another equation relating $A$ and $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{(1)}^{2}+2(1)-1 & =A{(3(1)-1)}^{2}+B(3(1)-1)+C \\\\ 10 & =4A+2B+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 2A+B & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding our two equations $A-B=-\\dfrac{5}{3}$ and $2A+B=\\dfrac{14}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3A & =-\\frac{5}{3}+\\frac{14}{3} \\\\ 3A & =3 \\\\ A & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into $2A+B=\\dfrac{14}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1)+B & =\\frac{14}{3} \\\\ B & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9x^2+2x-1}{(3x-1)^3} = \\dfrac{1}{(3x-1)} +\\dfrac{8}{3(3x-1)^2} + \\dfrac{2}{3(3x-1)^3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{9{x}^{2}+2x-1}{(3x-1)^{3}}\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{(3x-1)}+\\frac{8}{3(3x-1)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{3(3x-1)^{3}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{3x-1}\\text{d}x+\\frac{8}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{(3x-1)^{2}}\\text{d}x+\\frac{2}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{(3x-1)^{3}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-1|+\\frac{8}{3}(-\\,\\frac{1}{3(3x-1)})+\\frac{2}{3}(-\\,\\frac{1}{6(3x-1)^{2}})+K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-1|-\\frac{8}{9(3x-1)}-\\frac{1}{9(3x-1)^{2}}+K\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042676/example-509.json" "file_sha256" "c1b4a3a046420671952d88ed307528ee3fdeab7ee40cb620264db559f1299af7"} {"question" "q-11340" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+\\frac{5}{x+2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+K"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042676/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "62be83fb3cd0e8c57c598071e3a6efb99cb487220147ae75aee6149023f94333" "reviewer_session" "01a108cd-e7f4-73f3-b8fc-c82135bc6b7d" "rationale" "Partial fractions give −2/(x+2)−5/(x+2)²+2/(x−3). Integrating yields −2ln|x+2|+5/(x+2)+2ln|x−3|+K on intervals excluding −2 and 3. The original screenshot shows two separate absolute-value arguments, exactly option a (index 0). The authentic worked solution and saved Correct grade confirm it. The database vmatrix combines unrelated terms into a malformed determinant; a global rule equating that structure with separate absolute values would not preserve mathematical scope." "bindings" {"problem" "ee2f89584c73e91ef373773cb98d13d4af9201c2b876af23c06e7096b58dff93" "worked_soluti