11340" [] "e-7298" [] "e-509" [] "e-7275" [] "q-82892" [] "e-508" []} "basis" 1577 "retractions" [[17592186068946 :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\n$∫(5x + 35)/((x + 2)^{2}(x - 3))dx =$"] [17592186068946 :question/answer-fields 17592186065470] [17592186069458 :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\n$∫(3x + 1)/((x + 1)^{3})dx =$"] [17592186068511 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{2x+3}{(x+1)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x$."] [17592186068511 :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{(x+1)^2} = \\dfrac{A}{(x+1)} +\\dfrac{B}{(x+1)^2}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A$, and $B$, we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x+1)^2$ and get\n\n$$\n2x+3 = A(x+1)+B\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-1$, we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(-1) + 3 &= A(-1 + 1) + B \\\\\n1 &= 0 + B \\\\\nB &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B$, we can solve for $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(0) + 3 &= A(0 + 1) + B \\\\\n3 &= A + B \\\\\n3 &= A + 1 \\\\\nA &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{(x+1)^2} = \\dfrac{2}{(x+1)} +\\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{1}(2x + 3)/((x + 1)^{2})dx &= ∫_{0}^{1}((2)/((x + 1)) + (1)/((x + 1)^{2}))dx \\\\\n&= 2∫_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x + 1}dx + ∫_{0}^{1}(1)/((x + 1)^{2})dx \\\\\n&= 2\\ln \\mid x + 1 \\mid \\mid _{0}^{1} + (-\\frac{1}{x + 1})\\begin{vmatrix}_{0}^{1} \\\\ = 2\\ln\\end{vmatrix}x + 1 \\mid \\mid _{0}^{1} - \\frac{1}{x + 1} \\mid _{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= 2[\\ln 2 - \\ln 1] - [\\frac{1}{2} - 1] \\\\\n&= 2\\ln 2 + \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186068567 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{3x^2-5x+8}{(x-3)^2(x+1)}\\textrm{d}x$."] [17592186068567 :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3x^{2} - 5x + 8)/((x - 3)^{2}(x + 1)) &= (A)/((x - 3)) + (B)/((x - 3)^{2}) + (C)/((x + 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A$, $B$, and $C$, we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-3)^2(x+1)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x^{2} - 5x + 8 &= A(x - 3)(x + 1) + B(x + 1) + C(x - 3)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=3$, we can eliminate $A$ and $C$ and solve for $B$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3(3^{2}) - 5(3) + 8 &= A(3 - 3)(3 + 1) + B(3 + 1) + C(3 - 3)^{2} \\\\\n20 &= B(4) \\\\\nB &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-1$, we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3(-1)^{2} - 5(-1) + 8 &= A(-1 - 3)(-1 + 1) + B(-1 + 1) + C(-1 - 3)^{2} \\\\\n16 &= C(-4)^{2} \\\\\nC &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known values of $B$ and $C$, we can solve for $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3(0^{2}) - 5(0) + 8 &= A(0 - 3)(0 + 1) + B(0 + 1) + C(0 - 3)^{2} \\\\\n8 &= -3A + B + 9C \\\\\n8 &= -3A + 5 + 9(1) \\\\\n3A &= 6 \\\\\nA &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3x^2-5x+8}{(x-3)^2(x+1)} = \\dfrac{2}{(x-3)} +\\dfrac{5}{(x-3)^2} +\\dfrac{1}{(x+1)}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫(3x^{2} - 5x + 8)/((x - 3)^{2}(x + 1))dx &= ∫((2)/((x - 3)) + (5)/((x - 3)^{2}) + (1)/((x + 1)))dx \\\\\n&= 2∫\\frac{1}{x - 3}dx + 5∫(1)/((x - 3)^{2})dx + ∫\\frac{1}{x + 1}dx \\\\\n&= 2\\ln\\begin{vmatrix}x - 3 & + 5(-\\frac{1}{x - 3}) + \\ln & x + 1 & + K \\\\ = 2\\ln & x - 3 & - (5)/((x - 3)) + \\ln & x + 1\\end{vmatrix}+ K\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."] [17592186068566 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1} \\dfrac{x}{x^2+2x+1} \\,\\textrm{d}x$."] [17592186068566 :question/worked-solution "Let's first factor the denominator of the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{x^2+2x+1} = \\dfrac{x}{(x+1)^2}\n$$\n\nWe must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{(x+1)^2} = \\dfrac {A}{x+1} + \\dfrac {B}{(x+1)^2}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A$ and $B$, we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x+1)^2$ and get\n\n$$\nx= A(x+1)+B\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-1$, we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-1 &= A(-1 + 1) + B \\\\\nB &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B$, we can solve for $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0 &= A(0 + 1) + B \\\\\n0 &= A + B \\\\\n0 &= A - 1 \\\\\nA &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{(x+1)^2} = \\dfrac {1}{x+1} - \\dfrac {1}{(x+1)^2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{x^{2} + 2x + 1}dx &= ∫_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{x + 1} - (1)/((x + 1)^{2}))dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x + 1}dx - ∫_{0}^{1}(1)/((x + 1)^{2})dx \\\\\n&= \\ln \\mid x + 1 \\mid \\mid _{0}^{1} + \\frac{1}{x + 1}\\begin{vmatrix}_{0}^{1} \\\\ = (\\ln\\begin{vmatrix}1 + 1 & -\\ln\\end{vmatrix}0 + 1\\end{vmatrix}) + (\\frac{1}{1 + 1} - \\frac{1}{0 + 1}) \\\\\n&= (\\ln 2 - \\ln 1) + (\\frac{1}{2} - 1) \\\\\n&= \\ln 2 - \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186068512 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{9x^2+2x-1}{(3x-1)^3}\\textrm{d}x$."] [17592186068512 :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(9x^{2} + 2x - 1)/((3x - 1)^{3}) &= (A)/((3x - 1)) + (B)/((3x - 1)^{2}) + (C)/((3x - 1)^{3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A$, $B$, and $C$, we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(3x-1)^3$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9x^{2} + 2x - 1 &= A(3x - 1)^{2} + B(3x - 1) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nwe can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9(\\frac{1}{3})^{2} + 2(\\frac{1}{3}) - 1 &= A(3(\\frac{1}{3}) - 1)^{2} + B(3(\\frac{1}{3}) - 1) + C \\\\\nC &= \\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $C$, we get an equation relating $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9(0)^{2} + 2(0) - 1 &= A(0 - 1)^{2} + B(0 - 1) + C \\\\\n- 1 &= A - B + \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\nA - B &= -\\frac{5}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1$ and substituting the known value of $C$, we get another equation relating $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9(1)^{2} + 2(1) - 1 &= A(3(1) - 1)^{2} + B(3(1) - 1) + C \\\\\n10 &= 4A + 2B + \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\n2A + B &= \\frac{14}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding our two equations\n\n$$\nA-B=-\\dfrac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n2A+B=\\dfrac{14}{3}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3A &= -\\frac{5}{3} + \\frac{14}{3} \\\\\n3A &= 3 \\\\\nA &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into\n\n$$\n2A+B=\\dfrac{14}{3}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(1) + B &= \\frac{14}{3} \\\\\nB &= \\frac{8}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9x^2+2x-1}{(3x-1)^3} = \\dfrac{1}{(3x-1)} +\\dfrac{8}{3(3x-1)^2} + \\dfrac{2}{3(3x-1)^3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫(9x^{2} + 2x - 1)/((3x - 1)^{3})dx &= ∫((1)/((3x - 1)) + (8)/(3(3x - 1)^{2}) + (2)/(3(3x - 1)^{3}))dx \\\\\n&= ∫\\frac{1}{3x - 1}dx + \\frac{8}{3}∫(1)/((3x - 1)^{2})dx + \\frac{2}{3}∫(1)/((3x - 1)^{3})dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\ln\\begin{vmatrix}3x - 1 & + \\frac{8}{3}(- (1)/(3(3x - 1))) + \\frac{2}{3}(- (1)/(6(3x - 1)^{2})) + K \\\\ = \\frac{1}{3}\\ln & 3x - 1\\end{vmatrix}- (8)/(9(3x - 1)) - (1)/(9(3x - 1)^{2}) + K\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186065471 17592186065472 17592186065473 17592186065474 17592186065475 17592186068280 17592186068281 17592186068282 17592186068283 17592186068284] "immutable_fields" [[17592186065470 :answer-field/correct] [17592186